Working Principle

Introduction

The Arduino LED Pattern Controller using the 74HC595 Shift Register and Push Button demonstrates how multiple LEDs can be controlled efficiently using only three Arduino digital pins. The project uses serial communication to send data from the Arduino to the shift register, which converts it into parallel outputs for the LEDs.

---

System Overview

The project consists of four main sections:

1. Arduino Uno
2. 74HC595 Shift Register
3. Push Button
4. LED Output Section

The Arduino acts as the controller, the 74HC595 expands the available output pins, the push button changes the display mode, and the LEDs show the selected pattern.

---

Power Supply

The Arduino is powered through the USB cable.

The Arduino provides:

- 5V to the 74HC595 VCC pin.
- GND to the 74HC595 GND pin.

The MR (Master Reset) pin is connected to 5V to keep the shift register active.

The OE (Output Enable) pin is connected to GND so that all outputs remain enabled.

---

Data Communication

The Arduino communicates with the 74HC595 using three pins:

- DS (Data Pin) – Sends serial data.
- SH_CP (Shift Clock) – Shifts each bit into the register.
- ST_CP (Latch Clock) – Updates all outputs simultaneously.

The Arduino uses the "shiftOut()" function to send 8-bit binary data to the shift register.

---

LED Control

The eight output pins of the 74HC595 are connected to eight LEDs through 220 Ω resistors.

Each binary bit controls one LED.

Example:

- "1" → LED ON
- "0" → LED OFF

Example pattern:

10101010

This turns ON alternate LEDs.

---

Push Button Operation

A push button is connected to Arduino Digital Pin D2.

The button is configured using INPUT_PULLUP.

Button Released

- Arduino reads HIGH.

Button Pressed

- Arduino reads LOW.

Whenever the button is pressed, the Arduino changes to the next LED animation mode.

---

LED Animation Modes

The project contains multiple LED patterns.

Mode 1

Running LED (Left → Right)

Mode 2

Running LED (Right → Left)

Mode 3

Blink All LEDs

Mode 4

Alternate LEDs

Each button press selects the next mode.

---

Role of the Shift Register

The 74HC595 converts serial data received from the Arduino into parallel outputs.

This allows eight LEDs to be controlled using only three Arduino pins, saving GPIO pins and making the design expandable.

---

Advantages

- Saves Arduino output pins.
- Reduces wiring complexity.
- Easy to expand by cascading multiple 74HC595 ICs.
- Simple and low-cost design.
- Suitable for embedded systems learning.

